---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/advanced/torts-and-liability">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced lesson in Law, building directly on the civil–criminal divide you have
    already mastered. <em>What Makes a Law</em> (foundational) established what a legal
    rule is; <em>Criminal vs Civil Law</em> (intermediate) drew the line this lesson now works
    inside — the same event, two proceedings; and <em>How a Case
    Moves Through Court</em> (intermediate) explained the machinery a tort claim travels
    through. Here you meet the common law's most litigated formula — and run it.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state the four elements of negligence — duty, breach, causation, damages — and what each asks;</li>
    <li>apply the neighbour principle and the Caparo test to decide whether a duty exists;</li>
    <li>price a risk with the Hand formula and predict the liability outcome either way;</li>
    <li>explain the doctrines that rescue claimants where but-for causation fails;</li>
    <li>map the rest of tort — occupiers, nuisance, products, vicarious liability, defamation — and its defences.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Torts are civil wrongs other than breach of contract — and their modern centre of gravity is
    <strong>negligence</strong>: liability for harm caused by falling below the standard of care
    the situation demands. The test (duty, breach, causation, damage) is the most litigated
    formula in the common law, and understanding it means understanding how societies allocate
    the costs of accidents between strangers: which risks are reasonable, which losses are
    compensable, and who pays when careful people harm each other anyway.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a tort case and a criminal case are two names for the same proceeding. They differ in claimant (the injured party sues; the state prosecutes), standard of proof (balance of probabilities versus beyond reasonable doubt) and outcome (compensation versus punishment) — which is why the same event can end in an acquittal and a damages award, and why a negligence claim can proceed even where prosecutors decline to charge.</p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the negligence test, step by step — from the snail in the bottle to modern practice" widget="equationStep" config={tortCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> click "Walk me through it" and let the four steps arrive
    one at a time — the order is the order of argument in every negligence trial, and a claim
    that fails at step 2 never reaches step 3. Each note names the doctrine the corresponding
    section below unpacks, so use the widget as a map and return to it as you read.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> tort is a civil liability regime, and negligence is its centre of
    gravity — a four-element test, not a feeling that someone was careless. Now we walk the
    elements, starting where every claim starts: was a duty owed at all?
  </p>

  <h2>Duty of care: from snail to statute</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Donoghue v Stevenson</em> (1932) is tort's origin myth because it is genuinely
    foundational: a consumer, no contract with anyone (her friend bought the bottle), poisoned by
    a decomposed snail inside a sealed drink — and Lord Atkin's neighbour principle
    ("you must take reasonable care to avoid acts or omissions which you can reasonably foresee
    would be likely to injure your neighbour — persons so closely and directly affected by my act
    that I ought to have them in contemplation") generalised a duty from manufacturer to
    ultimate consumer. The modern English test (Caparo, 1990) is tripartite: foreseeability of
    harm, <strong>proximity</strong> of relationship, and whether imposing the duty is
    <em>fair, just and reasonable</em> — the last limb is where the courts do their real policy
    work, declining duties where liability would crush socially useful risk-taking. The
    boundary doctrines map the limit: <strong>no liability for pure omissions</strong> as a rule
    (no duty to rescue a drowning stranger — the common law's individualist signature, versus the
    duty-to-rescue statutes of much of continental Europe), <strong>psychiatric injury</strong>
    claims restricted to foreseeable psychiatric harm with defined classes of claimant
    (secondary victims face controlled thresholds), and <strong>pure economic loss</strong>
    largely excluded from negligence (the floodgates logic — a builder's negligent delay damages
    wallets, not bodies, and markets are expected to price that risk by contract; Hedley Byrne's
    negligent-misstatement exception carves the dutiful cases out). Read the exceptions and you
    are reading a theory of which harms the tort system is designed to handle.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each claim by whether a duty follows" widget="sort-buckets" config={tortDutyBuckets} />

  <h2>Breach: the standard of care, priced</h2>
  <p>
    Breach asks what the reasonable person would have done in the defendant's position — an
    objective standard with calibrated adjustments: higher for professionals (Bolam: what a
    responsible body of the profession would do — plus, post-<em>Montgomery</em> (2015), what
    the individual patient would find material, the informed-consent revolution in medical law),
    lower for children, higher for those with expertise, indifferent to the defendant's
    inexperience. The operative logic was priced by Judge Learned Hand: <strong>liability where
    the burden of precaution (B) is less than the probability of harm times its magnitude
    (P × L)</strong>. <strong>Worked example:</strong> a warehouse's flood risk: loss £2m,
    probability 2% per year without a £50,000 defence. Expected harm = 0.02 × £2,000,000 =
    £40,000; B (£50,000) exceeds it, so <em>not</em> installing the defence is reasonable — no
    breach. Flip the numbers (probability 4%: expected harm £80,000) and failing to install is
    negligence. US courts cite the formula explicitly; English judges apply its logic without
    the arithmetic (<em>Bolton v Stone</em>: cricket ball over a high fence, tiny probability,
    no breach; <em>Miller v Jackson</em>: same ground, more houses built, breach found) — the
    difference between the two outcomes is the formula wearing wigs. <strong>Res ipsa
    loquitur</strong> ("the thing speaks for itself") shifts the evidential burden where the
    accident is of a kind that ordinarily does not happen without negligence.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="price the precaution — breach begins where expected harm crosses the burden" widget="plotter" config={tortHandPlot} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "negligence means carelessness, so someone must pay." All four
    elements must be satisfied and the standard is conduct-relative: a careful driver who skids
    on black ice no one could foresee owes nothing, and a foreseeable loss caused by conduct
    meeting the standard is nobody's tort. Tort is a liability regime about <em>unreasonable</em>
    risk, not a general accident-compensation scheme — the gap between the two is what no-fault
    systems (below) were invented to close.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="true or false — who actually pays" widget="judge" config={tortCarelessJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> duty exists or it does not, and breach is a priced judgment about
    unreasonable risk — conduct-relative, not outcome-based. But even an unreasonable act is not
    actionable unless it <em>caused</em> the harm — the system's hardest problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Causation: the system's hardest problem</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Factual causation</strong> runs the but-for test: but for the breach, would the harm
    have occurred? <strong>Legal causation</strong> then trims: remoteness (foreseeable kinds of
    harm — the <strong>eggshell-skull rule</strong> takes the victim as found: unforeseeable
    <em>extent</em> does not limit liability) and <strong>novus actus interveniens</strong> (a
    new intervening act — especially the claimant's own grossly unreasonable response — breaks
    the chain). The system's real sophistication shows at the edges where but-for fails.
    <strong>Multiple sufficient causes</strong> (each exposure alone could have caused the
    disease): the <strong>Fairchild exception</strong> (mesothelioma from multiple employers'
    asbestos) substitutes material-increase-in-risk liability, with the Mesothelioma Act 2008
    turning it into a statutory compensation route — law bending its causal concept around
    epidemiological reality. <strong>Material contribution to injury</strong> (Bonnington: dust
    from guilty and innocent sources both accumulating) and <strong>loss of a chance</strong>
    (medical prognoses, commercial advice — recover the percentage value of the chance destroyed)
    complete the toolkit. Every doctrine here is an answer to one question: when science cannot
    say which of many causes did it, who should bear the uncertainty — and English law's answers
    are case-specific compromises, honestly described as such.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each causation doctrine with its patch" widget="match" config={tortCauseMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the negligence formula is complete — duty, breach priced by the
    Hand logic, causation patched by honest compromises where science cannot decide. The rest
    of tort is a family of older, narrower wrongs, each shifting the liability trigger in a way
    worth noticing.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond negligence: the rest of the tort map</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Occupiers' liability</strong>: premises duties to visitors (statutory — high for
    lawful visitors, lower for trespassers, child-proof in effect: a child trespasser is
    attracted by what attracts children — the doctrine of allurement).</li>
    <li><strong>Nuisance and the escape of dangerous things</strong>: unreasonable interference
    with land (private) or public rights (public), and Rylands v Fletcher's strict liability for
    escapes from non-natural land use — the ancestors of environmental liability.</li>
    <li><strong>Product liability</strong>: the Consumer Protection Act 1987 imposes
    <strong>strict liability</strong> (no proof of fault) on producers for defective products —
    the EU-harmonised regime where risk-creation, not carelessness, is the trigger.</li>
    <li><strong>Vicarious liability</strong>: employers answer for employees' torts in the
    course of employment — modern cases extended it to relationships "akin to employment" and
    even some unauthorised acts (the various sex-abuse institutional-liability cases), because
    the enterprise that profits from the activity should carry its risks.</li>
    <li><strong>Defamation</strong>: false statements damaging reputation, with the statutory
    defences (truth, honest opinion, publication on a matter of public interest) and the
    serious-harm threshold of the Defamation Act 2013 — the permanent tension between
    reputation and speech, resolved jurisdiction by jurisdiction (English libel law's
    claimant-friendly history, and its reform).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Damages and defences</h2>
  <p>
    Compensation is the principle: <strong>special damages</strong> (quantified pecuniary loss —
    earnings, care costs, with future loss converted to lump sums via multipliers and discount
    rates), <strong>general damages</strong> (pain, suffering, loss of amenity — tariffed by
    judicial guidelines), and rarely <strong>aggravated or exemplary damages</strong> (English
    law restricts punitive awards to defined categories — oppressive state action, profit from
    wrongdoing). Defences shape the same ledger: <strong>contributory negligence</strong>
    (claimant's share reduces damages by percentage — the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence)
    Act 1945), <strong>volenti non fit iniuria</strong> (voluntary assumption of risk — narrow
    in practice, effectively barred in employment and road cases by statute), and
    <strong>illegality</strong> (the ex turpi doctrine, recalibrated by <em>Patel v Mirza</em>
    into a policy-based balancing). The system's deepest design fact: defendants are almost
    always insured and claims are priced actuarially — so tort functions in practice as a
    distributed insurance mechanism with fault-based gating, which is precisely the design
    question its critics raise.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="file each wrong by what triggers it" widget="sort-buckets" config={tortMapBuckets} />
  <Widget no="8" caption="find the line the courtroom drama wrote" widget="spot-error" config={tortDamagesSpot} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> exemplary (punitive) damages are a normal feature of tort awards, as imported courtroom drama suggests. English law confines them to narrow categories — oppressive action by public officials and profit calculated from wrongdoing — because the system's premise is compensation, not punishment; punishment is the criminal law's department. Damages here restore, and only rarely make a point.</p>

  <h2>Nuisance and Rylands: neighbours and escapes</h2>
  <p>
    Nuisance governs land's neighbourhood effects. <strong>Private nuisance</strong> protects
    an occupier's use and enjoyment against unreasonable interference (smells, noise,
    vibrations, encroaching roots) — unreasonableness judged by locality, duration, sensitivity
    (abnormally sensitive uses get less protection) and the gravity of harm weighed against
    the utility of the conduct; remedies run from injunction (the default for continuing
    interference) to damages. <strong>Public nuisance</strong> protects shared rights
    (obstructing a highway) and grounds representative claims. <strong>Rylands v
    Fletcher</strong> (1868) added strict liability: a landowner who brings something dangerous
    onto land (reservoir water, in the founding case) and it escapes, answers for the
    consequences without proof of negligence — the ancestor of modern environmental liability,
    its scope contested into the modern era (the "non-natural use" limit; exclusions for
    natural and statutory escapes) and partially absorbed into negligence and statutory
    regimes (the contaminated-land provisions of the Environmental Protection Act). The
    doctrinal trajectory is a case study in how the common law absorbs industrialisation: from
    neighbourly wrongs to risk-based liability, with each new technology — railways,
    chemicals, genetically modified crops — stress-testing the categories.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drag the divider — private, then public" widget="compare" config={tortNuisanceCompare} />

  <h2>Defamation: reputation's protection and its limits</h2>
  <p>
    Defamation balances reputation against expression, and its modern English shape (Defamation
    Act 2013) shows the balance re-weighted. Elements: a defamatory statement about an
    identifiable claimant, published to a third party — with the <strong>serious harm</strong>
    threshold screening trivial claims, and the single-publication rule ending the
    archive-republication problem. Defences: <strong>truth</strong> (the only absolute
    answer), <strong>honest opinion</strong> (protected if indicated as opinion and grounded
    in facts genuinely believed), <strong>public interest</strong> (the Reynolds heritage
    codified — responsible journalism on matters of public concern, judged by editorial
    conduct), plus privilege (parliamentary and court proceedings, absolute) and the
    offer-of-amendment machinery. The comparative contrast is the lesson: US law's
    actual-malice requirement (<em>New York Times v Sullivan</em> — public figures must prove
    knowing falsity) makes defamation nearly unreachable for public-figure plaintiffs, while
    English law's claimant-friendliness historically attracted libel tourism — prompting the
    2013 reforms and the anti-SLAPP movement (against strategic suits aimed at silencing
    participation) now legislating in several jurisdictions. The deep tension is permanent:
    reputation's protection is speech's limit, and every jurisdiction re-draws the line with
    its own politics.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="bet on the two libel regimes" widget="predict" config={tortDefamePredict} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the four elements of negligence in order, and say in one phrase what each element asks.</li>
    <li>A hazard threatens a loss of 1,000,000 with probability 3% a year; a precaution costing 25,000 would remove it. Apply the Hand formula — is failing to take the precaution a breach?</li>
    <li>The remoteness rule limits liability to foreseeable <em>kinds</em> of harm, yet the eggshell-skull rule imposes liability for unforeseeable <em>extent</em>. Explain the distinction the pair of doctrines draws, and why it is not a contradiction.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Duty — was a legal obligation of care owed at all (neighbour
    principle, Caparo's three limbs)? Breach — did the conduct fall below the standard of the
    reasonable person, priced by B against P times L? Causation — did the breach factually and
    legally produce the harm (but-for, then remoteness and intervening acts)? Damages — what
    money restores the claimant? (2) Expected harm is 0.03 × 1,000,000 = 30,000, and the
    burden of precaution is 25,000: B is less than P times L, so liability attaches — failing
    to take the precaution is a breach. Notice this is the flipped warehouse example: same
    formula, opposite verdict.
    (3) Remoteness asks <em>what kind</em> of harm a reasonable defendant could have
    contemplated — foreseeability of the kind is what links the wrong to the injury. The
    eggshell-skull rule then refuses to let the defendant off because the harm turned out
    <em>worse</em> than expected: you take your victim as found. There is no contradiction: the
    two rules operate on different variables — type foreseen, extent borne — so the defendant
    answers for the whole of a foreseeable kind of harm, however fragile the claimant.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Tort is civil wrongs outside contract; negligence — duty, breach, causation, damages — is its litigated centre.</li>
    <li>Duty is generalised by the neighbour principle and policed by Caparo's foreseeability, proximity and policy limbs.</li>
    <li>Breach is priced: liability where the burden of precaution is less than probability times loss.</li>
    <li>Causation runs but-for, trimmed by remoteness and intervening acts — with Fairchild, material contribution and loss of a chance as honest patches.</li>
    <li>Outside negligence, the trigger shifts: strict liability for products and escapes, vicarious liability for enterprises, statutory duties for occupiers.</li>
    <li>Damages restore rather than punish; contributory negligence, volenti and illegality shape the ledger.</li>
    <li>In practice tort is a distributed insurance mechanism with fault-based gating — hence the no-fault alternatives on the table.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Contracts</em> (advanced) is tort's sibling obligation — tort imposes duties on
    strangers, contract enforces promises between parties, and the boundary cases (negligent
    misstatement, economic loss) live where the two meet. <em>Property Law</em>
    (advanced) carries the land-based wrongs of this lesson — nuisance, Rylands, occupiers'
    duties — into the law of ownership itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The economic programme and its critics.</strong> Calabresi and Posner reframed tort
    as optimal accident-cost allocation: the cheapest cost avoider should bear the risk, liability
    rules should minimise the sum of accident and prevention costs, and strict liability belongs
    where activity levels (not just care levels) drive risk. The programme's influence on
    doctrine is real (risk calculus in breach, insurance reasoning in vicarious liability); its
    critics answer that dignity, corrective justice (Weinrib) and the expressive function of law
    resist reduction to arithmetic — a live jurisprudential fight with practical stakes
    (capping damages, no-fault schemes). <strong>System alternatives:</strong> New Zealand
    abolished most accident tort claims in favour of a universal accident-compensation scheme —
    the standing natural experiment showing compensation without fault litigation; UK employers'
    liability and motor insurance are already quasi-no-fault in operation. <strong>Open
    questions:</strong> who is liable when an AI system errs (developer, deployer, data
    provider — the EU's AI-liability directives and product-liability reform are the first
    doctrinal answers); how tort meets autonomous vehicles (Hand's formula assumes a human
    choosing precaution); whether the tort system's transaction costs (most premium pounds paid
    out in costs, not compensation) are reformable without abandoning fault; and how privacy
    torts (misuse of private information — the English invention that grew around the old
    breach-of-confidence action) will handle the platform era, where the harm is diffuse,
    the injury statistical, and the defendant a business model.
  </p>
  <p>
    The system's aggregate performance deserves its own measure: transaction costs.
    Personal-injury compensation flows through insurers and lawyers, with a substantial share
    of premiums absorbed by administration — the recurring argument for no-fault schemes
    (New Zealand's, above) and for streamlined claims processes. The counter-argument — that
    fault liability deters unsafe conduct in ways compensation schemes cannot — keeps the
    debate open. Both sides agree the current system is neither cheap nor perfectly
    deterrent; they disagree about which function to optimise, and the disagreement is
    ultimately about what tort is <em>for</em>: compensation, deterrence, corrective justice
    between the parties, or all three at prices societies have never fully audited.
  </p>

